equality act now  in february the vast majority of  republicans in the house of  representatives  voted against the equality act the  equality act was  the goal was to expand lgbtq  protections and if you look at sec  filings you will see that  a lot of the best known companies in the  united states and that includes the ones  i mentioned  donated a whole bunch of money to  politicians who voted against the bill  now most companies donate to both  political parties you look at you know  comcast you look at these big isps  you look at a lot of the big social  media platforms you look at all of it  most of them are donating to both sides  why well it's sort of obvious you don't  know  as a corporation which way the political  wins are going to go  you could get involved and try to get if  you think democrats are better for your  business you could say let's try to get  democrats involved only but then what if  it doesn't happen  you're in a pretty awkward position as a  company that wants to  affect lobbying and legislative power  over uh members of congress etc so  you just donate to both sides and then  whoever wins wins and then you've got  hopefully friends regardless  and this is exactly what is going on  here and  you know i don't mind i think it's a  great thing  if from a marketing perspective it's  better to be pro-equality than  anti-equality right like  put the money out of it for a second if  you just say to me  would you prefer a country in which  saying  we celebrate pride month is good for  business  rather than a country in which saying no  gays allowed  is good for business of course the  country  in which it's more profitable to be for  equality  is the country that i prefer but these  companies  are just doing this for business reasons  in the companies there's no doubt that  you would be able to find  uh homophobic people and people who are  for lgbt rights and  are inclusive and welcoming and for  equality and all of that  but a corporation one of the things  about it is  that it takes on different dynamics than  simply the sum of the opinions of the  individuals who make it up and this is  how you get corporations  that end up doing completely immoral and  horrible things if you  individually talk to the people at a lot  of these companies and say hey  would you ever authorize the known  pollution of a river with carcinogenic  whatever  it'd be hard to find individuals who say  yeah that would be fine with me  but you end up with a corporation where  roles and responsibilities are  compartmentalized and you end up with  corporations  doing things or at least looking away as  things are done that individually many  of these people would not do  much the same way you could have  homophobic  and and uh lgbtq inclusive people at a  corporation and of course amazon does  and of course mcdonald's does at their  stores and of course walmart does  there's no doubt about it  but then as a corporation you would say  what is best for the bottom line and  what's best for the bottom line right  now  is you celebrate pride month brazenly  and cynically from the perspective of  what you really believe as a company  but you keep the money flowing to the  anti-gay  elected officials because the tide could  turn and they could end up  in power now um should you consider this  in deciding whether to do business with  walmart or amazon or mcdonald's  i don't know the answer to that i don't  know the answer to that because  certainly the fact that they're at least  celebrating pride month rather than  taking a homophobic position  is preferable but at the same time if it  really came down to it  and their profits depended only on  anti-gay politicians  then what would your position be what  would their position be  on pride month by the way so i don't  know i i my approach in thinking about  what companies i do business with  is if i'm doing business with a small  business  i do sort of pay attention to you know  if there's three owners there's a  there's restaurants i've found out  have completely xenophobic  owners um and i've stopped going to that  restaurant  with a company like amazon or walmart i  haven't been in a walmart in 15 20 years  or something like that  it's less about the individuals because  there's so many  individuals and i think it's a really  personal decision i don't really have  any advice on that but  the exact same cynicism with which i saw  the implicit bias trainings being rolled  out over the summer and into  early early fall last year we're seeing  the exact same thing from these  companies celebrating pride month  while under uh the table or but not  under the table behind the scenes  donating to elected officials who were  disgusted by the concept of pride month  let me know what you think  about how you decide whether such a  company is one  that you want to give your hard earned  dollars to i'm curious to hear from you  and we'll have more on this on our  instagram account you can find us on  instagram at davidpakmanshow  if you want to see me at the beach over  the weekend  for a break from politics follow me on  instagram  at david.pakman
 

